## SYNOPSIS

`secure-terminal` [OPTIONS] [`--` <PROGRAM> [<ARGS>...]]

`secure-terminal` `-e` <STRING>

`secure-terminal` `--reuse` [OPTIONS] [`--` <PROGRAM> [<ARGS>...]]

`secure-terminal` `ctl` [`--instance-group` <NAME>] <COMMAND> [<ARGS>...]

## DESCRIPTION

secure-terminal is a small terminal for pasting commands into, and reading text
out of, without worrying about invisible characters, bidi overrides or escape
sequences. Its default display mode, `detail`, is printable ASCII: a non-ASCII
character is NAMED inline as `<U+XXXX NAME>` rather than drawn, so a look-alike
reads as its identity. The other modes trade that differently -- `reveal` shortens
the badge to the codepoint, `box` replaces the character with one coloured
placeholder glyph, and `show` renders a printable glyph as itself (tinted by risk
class), which is the one mode that puts real non-ASCII on screen. What holds in
EVERY mode is that no invisible, bidi or control character reaches you unmarked.

It runs no escape-sequence parser on program output beyond safe colour and four
line-local edits within the line being written, so a pasted or printed lie cannot
reorder what you see or reach a line you have already scrolled past. Paste is
sanitized to ASCII before it reaches the shell.

Without `--` or `-e`, a shell (from `$SHELL`, else `/bin/bash`) is started. With
`--` the remaining arguments are run as a real argv, with no shell reparse.

Each launch opens its own independent window and process (see INSTANCES); pass
`--reuse` to add a tab to an already-running instance instead.

## OPTIONS

  * `--title` <TITLE>:
    Set the initial tab title.

  * `--tui`, `--no-tui`:
    Start this tab in TUI mode (interpret escapes through a confined screen
    model) or in the safe CLI mode. Overrides the configured default for this
    tab only. TUI mode needs `python3-pyte`.

  * `--mode` `box`|`show`|`reveal`|`detail`:
    Initial unicode display mode (see DISPLAY MODES).

  * `-e`, `--command` <STRING>:
    Run <STRING>, shell-split but without a shell. Prefer `--` <PROGRAM>
    <ARGS> for a real argv.

  * `--tab`:
    Start another per-tab option group: the options after it configure the next
    tab.

  * `--colors`, `--no-colors`, `--line-edits`, `--no-line-edits`, `--bell` <CHANNELS>, `--osc` <FEATURE>:
    Per-tab overrides of the configured defaults for the matching keys (see
    CONFIGURATION); an administrator `lock` still wins. `--osc` is repeatable.

  * `--class` <CLASS>:
    Set the window WM_CLASS / Wayland app-id, for window-manager rules.

  * `--name` <NAME>:
    Set the window WM_CLASS instance name (X11).

  * `--reuse`:
    Open a new tab in the group's running (primary) instance instead of a new
    window. Starts a fresh instance if none is running (see INSTANCES).

  * `--window`:
    Open a new independent window and process (the default). The explicit
    spelling of the default behaviour.

  * `--new-instance`:
    Force a standalone, server-less process: like the default new window, but it
    never becomes the group primary and answers no `ctl` or `--reuse`.

`--reuse`, `--window` and `--new-instance` are three mutually exclusive
dispositions; at most one may be given.

  * `--if-absent`:
    With `--reuse`, skip any tab whose command already runs in a tab of the
    target window, opening only the ones not present. The open request is
    idempotent: re-sending the same batch adds nothing. Matching is by command
    (a `-e` STRING and its shell-split argv are equal), so a renamed tab is
    still matched. No effect without `--reuse`.

  * `--instance-group` <NAME>:
    The instance group whose primary `--reuse` and `ctl` target (default:
    "default"). See INSTANCES.

  * `--clipboard-watch`, `--tray`:
    Run as a tray-only clipboard sanitizer instead of opening a terminal window.
    It watches the system clipboard and, when copied text carries deceptive
    Unicode (an invisible, bidirectional or control character, or a homoglyph
    posing as ASCII), offers to replace it with a safe version -- flag-and-offer,
    it never rewrites the clipboard on its own. This protects text later pasted
    into an editor or any program that does not neutralize Unicode. It sanitizes
    the LOCAL clipboard only; it does not alter the Qubes inter-VM global
    clipboard (Ctrl+Shift+C / Ctrl+Shift+V). See also `sclip`(1).

  * `--version`:
    Print the version and exit.

  * `-h`, `--help`:
    Print a usage summary and exit.

  * `--` <PROGRAM> [<ARGS>...]:
    Run <PROGRAM> directly as a real argv (no shell reparse). Repeat a leading
    program per tab where supported.

## DISPLAY MODES

secure-terminal has two rendering modes, chosen per tab:

  * CLI mode (default, safe):
    A strict, line-oriented display. The only escapes honored are safe colour and
    four line-local edits (cursor forward/back, cursor-to-column, erase-in-line),
    and only within the line being written; vertical and absolute movement is
    stripped. A program can redraw its own current line, exactly as a carriage
    return does, but can never reach back to a line already scrolled past. Those
    four are what tab completion needs (Tab makes the shell rewrite the line you
    are typing in place); `line_edits=false`, or **View > Line editing**, turns
    them off -- see the key's entry in the defaults file (see CONFIGURATION) for
    what that gains and costs.

  * TUI mode (opt-in):
    Escapes are interpreted inside an isolated in-memory screen model that has
    no OS reach: it cannot set the window title or touch the system clipboard.
    Every on-screen character is still ASCII/unicode-filtered and colours keep a
    contrast guard. Use it to run full-screen programs (ssh, an editor, htop) and
    for the shell's completion menus and progress displays.

The unicode `--mode` controls how a non-ASCII byte in output is shown (the top-bar
label for `detail` is "Detail"):

  * `box`:
    Replace it with a coloured box, one per character, tinted by risk class. On
    copy the box maps to a plain ASCII "_"; a saved transcript instead names each
    such byte inline (`<U+XXXX NAME>`), so the record stays lossless.

  * `show`:
    Render the glyph when printable, tinted by risk class so a look-alike stands
    out (a homoglyph confusable with ASCII wears a louder colour than honest
    foreign text). Box-drawing and block-element glyphs are structural, not
    deceptive, so they render in the program's own colour. A non-ASCII space is
    shown as a distinct marker and copies as "_", never as a space. Non-printable
    classes (invisible, bidi, control) have no glyph, so they still show as the same
    coloured box.

  * `reveal`:
    Show a `<U+XXXX>` codepoint badge (hover for its name).

  * `detail`:
    Like reveal but with the codepoint's Unicode name inline, `<U+XXXX NAME>`, so
    a homoglyph reads as its identity rather than a bare number (the safe default).

## REVIEW BAR

When text crossing the terminal boundary would carry unicode or control
characters -- a paste coming IN, or a selection copied OUT with Ctrl+C -- the tab
HOLDS it and shows a review bar docked at the bottom; no byte reaches the shell
(or the clipboard) until you choose. The bar shows a one-line summary, a
per-class breakdown of what is hidden (look-alikes, invisible/zero-width,
bidirectional controls, control characters, combining marks, box-drawing, other
non-ASCII), a structure summary (line count, whether it would run on its own,
length), and a mirror pane rendering exactly what each choice would deliver. The
held text is editable: trim the risky part and the summary, breakdown and mirror
update, and delivery sends exactly the edited buffer (re-sanitized, so an edit
cannot bypass the neutralization). Choose Reject, the ASCII form (look-alikes
stripped) or the unicode form (printable non-ASCII kept). A paste never runs on
its own: the trailing submit byte is removed so the command waits at the prompt
for your own Enter.

`paste_warn` and `copy_warn` (each `unicode` by default; also `always` or
`never`) control when the bar appears in each direction. The two directions are
NOT symmetric, by design:

  * A paste is inspected as the raw clipboard bytes, in every display mode.

  * A Ctrl+C copy is inspected as the ON-SCREEN selection. In the box, reveal and
    detail display modes the selection is already sanitized to ASCII, so nothing
    risky can reach the clipboard and no review is needed; only in show mode,
    where real glyphs are kept, does a copy raise the bar. A mouse selection (the
    X11 PRIMARY buffer) and drag-and-drop are always hard-stripped to ASCII, so a
    middle-click paste of a selection is safe without a review. This is why a copy
    may raise the bar while pasting the same text back does not: what reached the
    clipboard was already safe.

## CONFIGURATION

Settings are `KEY=value` lines spread over `*.conf` drop-in files, read from
these directories, lowest precedence first:

    /usr/lib/secure-terminal.d/*.conf       built-in vendor defaults
    /etc/secure-terminal.d/*.conf           system administrator
    /usr/local/etc/secure-terminal.d/*.conf local administrator
    ~/.config/secure-terminal.d/*.conf      user (highest precedence)

The user's own choices are written to
`~/.config/secure-terminal.d/50_user.conf`. A privileged directory may `lock` a
key so the user cannot override it. The built-in defaults file documents every
key. See also the app's View menu and the global Settings dialog.

## INSTANCES

Every launch opens a new independent window in its own process, like konsole or
qterminal -- a second `secure-terminal` gives you a second window, not a raised
first one. Independent processes keep sessions isolated and re-read the
administrator locks per process.

Reuse is opt-in. `--reuse` hands the launch to the group's PRIMARY instance,
which opens it as a new tab; with no tab options it opens a fresh default tab.
The primary is the FIRST live instance of a group: it owns the group's
owner-only socket and is what `--reuse` and `secure-terminal ctl` target. Later
launches in the same group run independently and do not disturb it. If no
primary is running, `--reuse` starts one.

`--instance-group` <NAME> selects the group (socket identity); an instance is
still a new window unless `--reuse` is also given. `--new-instance` forces a
standalone process that never becomes a primary and answers no `ctl` or
`--reuse`.

## REMOTE CONTROL

`secure-terminal ctl` can list tabs, rename a tab, read a tab's rendered text
(`dump-tab`), live-zoom a tab (`zoom`, no restart) and send text into a tab
(`send-text`, as if typed) over an owner-only socket. It is an injection/read surface and is OFF by default; it can
only be enabled from a privileged configuration directory (`remote_control`).

## FILES

  * `~/.config/secure-terminal.d/*.conf`:
    User configuration (see CONFIGURATION).

  * `~/.local/state/secure-terminal/`:
    Restored session: the tab list and each tab's scrollback.

  * `/etc/apparmor.d/secure-terminal`:
    AppArmor confinement profile.

## SECURITY

secure-terminal does not sandbox the programs you run; if you choose to run
something harmful it runs, as in any terminal. What it protects is VIEWING
untrusted data: a crafted file, a program's output, a login banner or a filename
can carry escape sequences that, on a normal terminal, act rather than merely
display -- quietly changing your clipboard (so a later paste inserts text you
never copied), drawing a link whose text lies about its target, or putting text
onto your input line. secure-terminal removes those escapes so reading untrusted
output is safe, and sanitizes paste so untrusted text cannot smuggle a hidden
command into the shell.

The safe-by-design guarantees rest on the CLI mode: no escape parser,
printable-ASCII-only display, ASCII-sanitized paste, and a memory-safe
implementation. TUI mode is a deliberate, clearly-indicated, lower-guarantee mode;
the confined screen model still filters every character, and each OSC feature
(title, clipboard, hyperlink, colours, ...) is neutralized unless you enable it
(see `View > OSC features`), at your own risk. Only run programs you trust in TUI
mode.

